flowfn/python/README.md:174:Both durable adapters share queue dependency, deduplication, retention, DLQ, backoff restoration and lease rules. They use the storage server clock for queue lease ownership. Queue state uses native per-job records; same-queue contention and dependency-heavy performance remain validation/implementation gates. PostgreSQL data survives client restarts; database persistence, backups and failover are deployment responsibilities. Consumer-group trimming preserves unread and pending deliveries just as Redis does.
flowfn/python/README.md:185:Use `.version('v1')` on the workflow builder and increment it whenever handler semantics change. A structural fingerprint also checks step names/types, parallel layout, delays and nested branches. `await workflow.resume_execution(execution_id)` claims an abandoned running execution after its lease expires; it rejects a different version/structure, an active owner, or a terminal execution. `.lease(milliseconds)` configures renewal/expiry (default 30 seconds). Lease clocks and atomic transitions belong to the storage adapter.
flowfn/python/README.md:200:Configure `flow.stream("events", {"max_length": 10000, "retention": 86400000})` to limit retained entries and age (`retention` is milliseconds). Policies run after publication and before subscription registration, stream information, range reads, and replay; an idle stream has no background cleanup timer. Explicit `stream.trim(TrimStrategy(max_age_seconds=...))` also supports fractional seconds. Redis and PostgreSQL use their storage clock for age, so client clock skew does not prematurely delete fresh entries. Unread and pending consumer-group deliveries remain protected even when they exceed a limit; acknowledgment makes them eligible for the next trim. Invalid limits are rejected before publishing.
flowfn/python/README.md:219:`get_stream_metrics(name)` reports retained messages and consumer-group lag (unread plus pending deliveries, summed across groups), alongside local publications in the current wall-clock second and average publish latency in milliseconds. Redis versions without group lag support return `None` for lag. Consumer counts are adapter-specific: memory reports active consumers, Redis reports registered consumer metadata, and PostgreSQL returns `None` because it has no durable consumer roster.
flowfn/python/README.md:235:`await flow.health_check(timeout=5.0)` probes the adapter even when no resources are registered. Redis and PostgreSQL return their storage clock; unavailable storage makes `healthy` false. The result includes persisted counts for registered queues/streams, local queue-worker counts, and running/stalled counts for registered workflow names. A running workflow with an absent or expired ownership lease is stalled, using the sampled storage time rather than the client clock. Resource read errors and stalled workflows make the overall result unhealthy. This is a sampled diagnostic, not an atomic snapshot or a discovery scan of all names in storage.
